THIRD PARTY
(a) Safety oversight of a third party should be part of the aerodrome operator’s safety
management system, that includes:
(1) establishing a formal agreement with the third party on the types and duration of the
contracted services and/or activities;
(2) administering a committee for the planning, monitoring and evaluating the contracted
services and/or activities;
(3) implementing risk management of the contracted services and/or activities;
(4) implementing performance monitoring of the contracted services and/or activities; and

(5) conducting audits and inspections of the contracted services and/or activities.

ESTABLISHING AND IMPLEMENTING A CRISIS MANAGEMENT PLAN
(a) A crisis management plan should include:
(1) strategic orders and instructions in dealing with different crisis situations;
(2) a crisis management centre located within the aerodrome;
(3) a crisis management centre has sufficient personnel, equipment and resources readily
available for use during an emergency;
(4) a crisis management group administered by the aerodrome operator for the strategic
response to a crisis; and
(5) a person responsible to lead the crisis management group.

ESTABLISHING STRATEGIC CRISIS MANAGEMENT CAPABILITY
(a) Guidance in establishing and implementing a crisis management plan at an aerodrome can be
referred to ISO 22361 – Security and Resilience: Crisis Management.
(b) Elements for consideration include:
(1) context, core concepts, principles and challenges;
(2) developing an organization’s crisis management capability;
(3) crisis leadership;
(4) the decision-making challenges and complexities facing a crisis team in action;
(5) crisis communication; and
(6) training, validation and learning from crises.
(c) It is applicable to top management with strategic responsibilities for the delivery of a crisis
management capability in any organization. It can also be used by those who operate under
the direction of top management.

BUSINESS CONTINUITY PLANNING ARRANGEMENTS
(a) Business continuity planning arrangements should include:
(1) a competent person responsible for establishing, implementing and maintaining all
aspects of business continuity planning at an aerodrome;
(2) a business continuity planning committee administered by the aerodrome operator
that:
(i) is lead under the direction of a senior management of the aerodrome operator;
(ii) includes representations from the agencies listed in the business continuity plan;
(iii) is responsible for preparing, accepting, and maintaining the business continuity
plan;
(iv) develops arrangements based on the identification and availability of agencies
and resources at, and in the vicinity of the aerodrome;
(v) establishes an exercise program to periodically test the business continuity plan;
(vi) review results from exercises and lessons from incidents/disruptions; and

(vii) ensure results and lessons contribute to continuous improvements to the
business continuity plan.

ESTABLISHING AND IMPLEMENTING A BUSINESS CONTINUITY PLAN
(a) Establishing and implementing a business continuity plan should include:
(1) recovery, resumption and maintenance of all aspects of the business;
(2) prioritization of business objectives and critical operations that are essential for
recovery of aerodrome services and/or activities;
(3) development and maintenance of business continuity plans and procedures;
(4) sufficient contingency plans to cope immediately with the full or partial loss of a critical
facility and infrastructure, which include but are not limited to runways, taxiways,
aprons, air traffic control tower, passenger terminals, freight facilities, fuel facilities,
road access, administration buildings and key facilities;
(5) determining the impact to key business processes and continuity of operations at an
aerodrome after an emergency or operational disruption; and
(6) sufficient resources to implement and maintain the plan.

EMERGENCY PLANNING ARRANGEMENTS
(a) Emergency planning arrangements should include:
(1) a competent person/s responsible for establishing, implementing and maintaining all
aspects of emergency planning and preparedness at an aerodrome;
(2) an emergency planning committee administered by the aerodrome operator, that:
(i) is lead under the direction of a senior member of the aerodrome certificate
holder;
(ii) includes representations from the agencies listed in the emergency plan;
(iii) is responsible for preparing, accepting, and maintaining the emergency plan;
(iv) develops emergency response arrangements based on the identification and
availability of emergency response agencies and resources at, and in the vicinity
of the aerodrome;
(v) establishes an exercise program to periodically test the emergency plan;
(vi) review results from emergency exercises and lessons from aviation
accidents/incidents; and
(vii) ensure results and lessons contribute to continuous improvements to the
emergency plan.

AEP COMPETENT PERSON
(a) It is important that a competent person is appointed with the overall responsibility to oversee
and manage all aspects of aerodrome emergency planning and preparedness. The responsible
person should have the necessary qualifications, competencies and experiences
commensurate with the size and complexity of the aerodrome, at least, with the following
knowledge, skills and understanding of:
(1) critical thinking skills;
(2) leadership and management skills;
(3) aerodrome emergency planning;
(4) business continuity management;
(5) crisis management;
(6) human factors in aviation;
(7) safety management system; and

(8) national and international standards and recommended practices.
(b) Depending on the size and complexity of the aerodrome, it may be necessary to form an
emergency planning team led by the responsible person to ensure effective emergency
planning, preparedness and management at an aerodrome.

EMERGENCY PLAN DOCUMENT
(a) An emergency plan document should, at least, include:
(1) an incident management system;
(2) types of emergencies planned for – aircraft emergencies, security-related, dangerous
goods, domestic fires, natural disasters, public health emergencies, etc.;
(3) on and off aerodrome agencies involved in the plan;
(4) roles and responsibilities of each agency for each type of emergency, the crisis
management centre, the emergency operations centre and the mobile command centre
including persons in-charge;
(5) information on names and telephone numbers of offices or people to be contacted in
the case of a particular emergency;
(6) emergency orders to deal with emergencies and emergency Instructions containing
separate sections for display by various persons or services concerned, e.g. air traffic
control, aerodrome rescue and firefighting service, police, ambulance, civil defence, etc;
(7) procedures for the safe management of persons evacuated from an aircraft or
aerodrome buildings into airside; and
(8) a grid map of the aerodrome and its immediate vicinity.

