EMERGENCY MEDICAL SERVICES, FACILITIES AND EQUIPMENT
(a) Emergency medical services, facilities and equipment at the aerodrome should include:
(1) sufficient procedures for managing evacuated aircraft passengers and crew to safe and
secure areas away from the scene of an incident in airside;
(2) rescue and firefighting personnel trained and competent in occupational first aid (OFA)
and cardiopulmonary resuscitation (CPR), as determined by an appropriate medical
person or organisation;
(3) where there are no medical services available at an aerodrome and/or it is anticipated
that a response from the nearest emergency medical services is more than 15 minutes,
at least, two full-time members per shift of the rescue and firefighting service or other
on-aerodrome personnel are trained and competent as qualified emergency medical
treatment level;
(4) sufficient emergency medical supplies are provided or made available within the vicinity
of the aerodrome, to render medical assistance commensurate with the level of
protection at an aerodrome;
(5) an assessment on the types and quantities of emergency medical supplies, facilities and
equipment to be provided at an aerodrome for emergency purposes; and

(6) emergency reception facilities including sufficient trained personnel and equipment are
available at an aerodrome including but not limited to:
(i) casualty collection and clearance centre;
(ii) aircraft crew reception centre;
(iii) family and friend reception centre;
(iv) survivor reunion centre;
(v) survivor reception centre;
(vi) survivor repatriation centre; and
(vii) temporary morgue (body holding area).

MULTI-RUNWAY AERODROME OPERATIONS
(a) Where the aerodrome operates more than one operational runway, an assessment of the
adequacy of the emergency plan in dealing with simultaneous aircraft emergencies occurring
at an aerodrome should be conducted, with the following considerations:
(1) design and layout of runways and taxiways;
(2) type of aircraft operations;
(3) type of aerodrome activities;
(4) adequacy of rescue and firefighting service provisions – personnel, appliances,
equipment and competency;
(5) availability of on and off aerodrome agencies’ resources and equipment;

(6) adequacy of supplementary water supply – location and capacities;
(7) emergency alerting and notification system;
(8) emergency communication network;
(9) adequacy of emergency reception facilities - location and size;
(10) triage and medical care protocols;
(11) incident management plans and procedures; and
(12) business continuity and recovery plans and procedures.

COORDINATION WITH OTHER LEGISLATIONS AND REGULATIONS
(a) The emergency plan should:
(1) coordinate with other applicable plans in compliance with the relevant legislations and
regulations:
(i) aerodrome security plan;
(ii) passenger welfare programme;
(iii) air accident investigation regulation;
(iv) aviation disaster family assistance plan;
(v) communicable disease management plan;
(vi) disabled aircraft removal plan;
(vii) search and rescue plan.; and
(2) ensure rescue and firefighting personnel and other emergency response personnel are
trained and competent on the preservation of evidences at aircraft accident scene.

REVIEW OF EMERGENCY PLAN DOCUMENT
(a) Policies and procedures should be implemented for managing subsequent changes to the
emergency plan, according to the aerodrome operator’s safety management system,
including:
(1) a systematic process to review, revise, and validate the emergency plan;
(2) a documentation control process;
(3) quality assurance and audit process;
(4) an approval process by the Accountable Manager or a delegated executive; and
(5) an updated plan provided to the GCAA.

TESTING AND EXERCISING THE EMERGENCY PLAN
(a) Testing and exercising the emergency plan should:
(1) commensurate with the types of aircraft operations and operational activities;
(2) ensure emergency exercises are sufficiently planned and managed with clear aims,
objectives, performance criteria and understood by all participant agencies;

(3) include the participation of on and off aerodrome agencies and its associated personnel
and resources;
(4) identify core elements of the emergency plan and established how and when these
elements are tested;
(5) ensure key aerodrome personnel participate in exercises as per their assigned strategic,
tactical and operational roles and responsibilities in the emergency plan;
(6) include safety assessment of emergency exercises in accordance against the agreed aim
and objectives of the exercise plan;
(7) where an aerodrome operates aircraft movement and other major operational activities
at night, conduct emergency exercises during the hours of darkness; and
(8) include records of the emergency exercises and ensure such records are readily
available for audit and inspection by the GCAA.

MODULAR TESTING
(a) Modular testing plan should, at least, include the following modules:
(1) emergency alerting and notification;
(2) rendezvous point management;
(3) mobile command post/operational command;

(4) emergency operations centre/tactical command;
(5) crisis management centre/strategic command;
(6) triage and medical care protocols;
(7) emergency reception centres/facilities;
(8) post-accident/preservation of evidence;
(9) business continuity/recovery operations; and
(10) full-scale emergency exercise.
(b) The implementation of a modular testing plan should be agreed with the GCAA and the
relevant AEP stakeholders.

MANAGING AERODROME EMERGENCY EXERCISE PLAN
(a) Policies and procedures should be implemented for the planning, conduct and review on the
effectiveness of the full-scale emergency exercise or modular testing plan, in accordance with
the aerodrome operator’s safety management system, including:
(1) a systematic process to review, revise, and validate the exercise plan;
(2) a documentation control process;
(3) quality assurance and audit process;
(4) an approval process by the Accountable Manager or a delegated executive; and
(5) submit the final exercise plan to the GCAA.

ASSESSMENTS FOR ACCIDENTS BEYOND RUNWAY THRESHOLDS (1 000 METRES RESPONSE AREA)
(a) Studies of the ICAO Accident Incident Data Reporting (ADREP) System show that a large (more
than 25 percent) portion of accidents occurred on the area (1 000 m long and 60 m width)
beyond the runway end. With this reason, an assessment of the approach and departure
areas within 1 000 m of the runway threshold should be carried out to determine the options
available for rescue, including suitable resources that should be provided. In considering the
need for any specialist rescue and access routes, the following factors should be considered:
(1) the environment, in particular the topography and composition of the surface;
(2) physical hazards and associated risks that exist within the area;
(3) options for access and for RFF purposes;
(4) hazards, risks and control measures of the options for rescue;
(5) use of external services;
(6) an analysis of the advantages and disadvantages of the options;
(7) policies and procedures to define and implement practices;
(8) competence standards to match the above; and
(9) monitoring testing and review of the capability.
(b) Aerodrome operator s should ensure the development of special procedures and availability
of equipment to deal with accidents or incidents that may occur in these areas. The facilities
that house this equipment need not be located on, or provided by, the aerodrome, if they can
be made available within reasonable time frames by off-aerodrome agencies as detailed in the
Aerodrome Emergency Plan.
(c) Where the 1 000 metres response area falls outside the aerodrome boundary and ARFFS is
required to respond to aircraft accidents or incidents using the public road and/or highway, an
assessment of the implications of such a response should be carried out. The following should
be considered:
(1) agreement with mutual-aid emergency services on the notification, mobilisation,
assignment of roles and responsibilities;
(2) legal requirements for ARFFS vehicles and drivers;
(3) suitable ARFFS policies and procedures are in place;
(4) competence and training requirements for ARFFS drivers;
(5) pre-planning of routes for suitability; and
(6) monitoring and review of such responses.

(d) Consideration also should be given to the following:
(1) provide direct access to the operational runway(s);
(2) designate access routes to the response area (consider debris and casualties);
(3) maintenance of roads and access routes (including construction activities);
(4) mitigate the possibility of any public and/or private non-emergency vehicle blocking the
progress of responding emergency vehicles;
(5) consider the gross weight and maximum dimensions of the ARFFS vehicle(s) expected to
use these routes/roads; or any other responding vehicles;
(6) that roads are capable of being traversed in expected conditions;
(7) exit/access gates or frangible sections in the security fence that are constructed to
allow ARFFS vehicles to safely pass through with minimal delay;
(8) exit/access points will need to be clearly identified. Retro-reflective tape or markers will
be of assistance where the aerodrome may need to be accessible during the hours of
darkness or conditions of low visibility;
(9) the mitigation of impediments to RFF vehicle mobility; and
(10) provide sufficient vertical clearance from overhead obstructions for the largest ARFFS
vehicles.

CHANGES IN LEVEL OF PROTECTION
(a) A change in level of protection should be expressed in terms of the new category of the
rescue and firefighting service available at an aerodrome;
(b) Where there is a permanent change in the level of protection, the aerodrome operator
should:
(1) notify the GCAA in writing;
(2) conduct a management of change;
(3) revise the task resource analysis; and
(4) update the aerodrome emergency plan.

SUPPLEMENTARY WATER SUPPLY SYSTEMS, FACILITIES AND EQUIPMENT
(a) Supplementary water supplies, facilities and equipment for the expeditious replenishment of
rescue and firefighting vehicles at an aircraft accident should be provided at the aerodrome.

(b) A fire water needs analysis should be conducted to determine additional amounts of water
required to replenish rescue and firefighting vehicles within five minutes immediately after an
aircraft accident, considering the following factors:
(1) sizes and types of aircraft using the aerodrome;
(2) the capacities and discharge rates of aerodrome fire vehicles;
(3) the provision of strategically located hydrants;
(4) the provision of strategically located static water supplies;
(5) utilization of existing natural water supplies for firefighting purposes;
(6) vehicle response times;
(7) historical data of water used during aircraft accidents;
(8) the need and availability of supplementary pumping capacity;
(9) the provision of additional vehicle-borne supplies;
(10) the level of support provided by local authority emergency services;
(11) the pre-determined response of local authority emergency services;
(12) fixed pumps where these may provide a rapid and less resource-intensive method of
replenishment;
(13) additional water supplies adjacent to aerodrome fire service training areas; and
(14) overhead static water supplies.
(c) Supplementary water supply storage and delivery facilities to be provided at an aerodrome
should include any of the following:
(1) a fire hydrant system (above, underground or combination);
(2) water supply vehicles; and/or
(3) water tanks.
(d) Supplementary water supply system, facilities and equipment should be suitably designed
with sufficient capacity in terms of amount, pressure and flow rate to meet the current and
future needs of the aerodrome to support immediate and protracted water flow requirements
in the event of an emergency.
(e) A preventive maintenance programme for the supplementary water supply system, facilities
and equipment should be established and implemented including:
(1) periodic inspection and testing of the system, facilities and equipment;
(2) a competent person to manage the maintenance programme; and
(3) records are maintained for at least 5 years, for audit and inspection by the GCAA.

PREVENTIVE MAINTENANCE OF RESCUE EQUIPMENT

1. RESCUE EQUIPMENT REQUIREMENTS
(a) The maintenance requirements for rescue equipment should be in accordance with original
equipment manufacturer (OEM) requirements. However, due to the nature of firefighting,
equipment can sometimes unknowingly become damaged. Consequently, it can also be
beneficial to check the following:
(1) all items — regular daily or weekly checks to ensure functionality;
(2) breathing apparatus sets — maintained after every use and checked regularly when not
used for safe operation;
(3) BA air quality — regularly checked (there may be national or local standards that the air
quality should meet);
(4) short lines/long lines (rescue lines) — not frayed and are in good repair;
(5) portable fire extinguishers — full and charged with pressure;
(6) fire hoses — inspected and pressure checked on an annual or six-monthly basis to
ensure that the hoses do not leak and the couplings are functioning and securely fitted;
(7) nozzles/foam branches — inspected for damage;
(8) rescue tools — inspected to ensure that there is no damage to components. Under high
forcing loads, damaged components can be very dangerous if they fail;
(9) general tools — inspected to ensure handles are not broken or damaged;
(10) first-aid kits — inspected at least weekly to ensure that items are maintained at the
correct stock levels; and
(11) rescue tool box — checked to ensure all tools are present.
(b) For detailed inspection and testing of rescue and firefighting equipment, should be in
accordance with Inspection and Testing of Equipment, UK Fire Service Manual – Volume 1:
Fire Service Technology, Equipment and Media.
2. MAINTENANCE DOCUMENTATION
(a) A complete set of maintenance documentation should be delivered with the rescue
equipment during the procurement process. As a minimum this should include:
(1) operating procedures;
(2) maintenance procedures;
(3) fault diagnosis and troubleshooting;
(4) adjustment procedures;
(5) removal/replacement of parts and repairable assemblies;
(6) instructions for disassembly and reassembly of repairable components;

(7) tolerances, specifications and capacities;
(8) illustrations and exploded views;
(9) schematic drawings, for example, electrical wiring circuits, pneumatic circuits, chassis
air circuits or hydraulic circuits;
(10)  special tools needed for repairing and adjusting; and
(11)  spare parts catalogue providing exploded views of the entire RFF vehicle.
(b) It is important that the technical documentation is in a format that can be easily read,
understood and followed.

RFFS COMPETENT PERSON

(a) It is important that the competent person responsible to oversee and manage a rescue and
firefighting service is subjected to a rigorous selection process by the aerodrome operator, in
accordance with CAR AGA PART ADR Post Holders.
(b) To ensure the competent person has the relevant qualifications and experience in managing a
rescue and firefighting service, commensurate with the size, complexity and risks at an
aerodrome, the person should, at least, demonstrate the following competencies:
(1) academic qualifications;
(2) relevant industry experience;
(3) strategic leadership and management;
(4) strategic and/or tactical incident command competence;
(5) aerodrome emergency planning including managing exercises;
(6) crisis management;
(7) human factors;
(8) safety management system;
(9) training and development;
(10) fire prevention; and
(11) national and international standards and recommended practices.

FITNESS PROGRAMME
(a) A medical fitness programme for rescue and firefighting personnel should include:
(1) a pre-employment evaluation for new hires;
(2) continued evaluation for in-service personnel:
(i) < below 40 years old, every 5 years;
(ii) 41 - 49 years old, every 2 years; and
(iii) > 50 years old, annually;
(3) a medical surveillance programme to identify any underlying medical conditions, which
may pose a risk to the RFF personnel; and
(4) a medical screening conducted by an authorised occupational medical person;
(b) A physical fitness programme for rescue and firefighting personnel should include:
(1) a pre-employment assessment for new hires and at least, an annual assessment for in-
service personnel to ensure an appropriate level of physical fitness is maintained;
(2) a fitness preparatory programme including sufficient fitness equipment and instructors;
and
(3) a suitable testing programme to assess the aerobic, anaerobic and flexibility level of all
personnel assigned for rescue and firefighting duties;
(c) A fitness standard and review committee should be established and implemented, that:
(1) determines appropriate physical fitness programmes including instructors, assessors,
facilities and equipment;
(2) determines the appropriate occupational medical evaluation programmes;
(3) monitors the implementation of fitness programmes; and

(4) assesses suitability of new hires and continuation of in-service personnel for operational
duties after returning from a long medical absence, suffering from serious illness,
medically unfit or demonstrating repetitive fitness failures.

LANDSIDE FIRE PREVENTION PROGRAMME
(a) A landside fire prevention programme at the aerodrome should include but not limited to:
(1) terminal buildings, cargo facilities, maintenance hangars and other ancillary structures
located within the aerodrome,
(2) identification on the types of fire risks and hazards present at these buildings and
facilities;
(3) control of hot works procedures and any types of works that may affect fire safety;
(4) provision of fire prevention equipment commensurate with the types of fire risks and
hazards;
(5) implementation of fire prevention inspection plans and procedures, commensurate
with the types of fire risks and hazards;
(6) provision of sufficient personnel to implement the programme;
(7) implementation of fire prevention promotional plans and procedures to educate
aerodrome community;
(8) implementation of fire investigation plans and procedures to identify the cause/s, and
prevent re-occurrence; and
(9) implementation of quality assurance plans and procedures to promote continuous
improvements to the programme.
(b) The fire prevention programme at the landside should comply with the applicable
requirements of the UAE fire code regulations, where necessary,

THIRD PARTY
(a) Safety oversight of a third party should be conducted as part of the holder’s safety
management system, at least, including:
(1) establishing a formal agreement with the third party on the types and duration of the
contracted services and/or activities;
(2) administering a committee for the planning, monitoring and evaluating the contracted
services and/or activities;
(3) implementing risk management of the contracted services and/or activities;
(4) implementing performance monitoring of the contracted services and/or activities; and

(5) conducting audits and inspections of the contracted services and/or activities.

EMERGENCY PLAN DOCUMENT
(a) Emergency plan document should be submitted to the GCAA for initial certification, at least,
with the following information:
(1) incident command structure;
(2) types of emergencies planned for;
(3) agencies involved in the plan;
(4) responsibility and role of each agency, the emergency operations centre and the
command post, for each type of emergency;
(5) information on names and telephone numbers of offices or people to be contacted in
the case of a particular emergency; and
(6) a grid map of the aerodrome and its immediate vicinity.
(b) Procedures should be implemented to ensure the emergency plan document is managed
according to the aerodrome operator’s safety management system, including:
(1) reviewing and updating the plan at least every two-years;
(2) the plan is approved by the Accountable Manager;
(3) ensuring quality assurance of the plan; and
(4) an updated copy is submitted to the GCAA.

MODULAR TESTING
(a) A modular testing approach should consider the following modules:
(1) emergency alerting and notification;
(2) rendezvous point management;

(3) mobile command post/operational command;
(4) emergency operations centre/tactical command;
(5) crisis management centre/strategic command;
(6) triage and medical care protocols;
(7) emergency reception centres/facilities;
(8) post-accident/preservation of evidence;
(9) business continuity/recovery operations; and
(10) full-scale emergency exercise.

FIRE RISK ASSESSMENT

(a) Guidance on conducting fire risk assessment can refer to GM AMC4 ACM.OPS.CERT.G.005(a)
Aerodrome works safety.

SUBPART E – GUIDANCE ON AERODROME DISRUPTION MANAGEMENT DURING CRISES
AND MAJOR OPERATIONAL DISRUPTIONS

Important Note:
(a) This guidance material is intended to supplement, and not replace, existing aerodrome crisis
management, business continuity, emergency planning, safety management, security,
facilitation, and operational contingency arrangements. Aerodrome operators should ensure
that disruption management is integrated into existing governance structures, documented
procedures, training programmes, exercises, and assurance processes.
(b) Disruption management should not be treated as a separate or isolated function. It should
serve as the operational coordination mechanism that enables the aerodrome operator to
manage abnormal conditions before they escalate into a crisis, while maintaining continuity of
critical services, protecting passengers and personnel, supporting emergency readiness, and
preserving safe and orderly airport operations.
(c) Aerodrome operators should apply this guidance proportionately, taking into account the size,
complexity, traffic volume, operational profile, stakeholder environment, and risk exposure of
the aerodrome. Where disruption conditions create strategic, national, reputational, safety,
security, or prolonged operational consequences, the aerodrome operator should escalate the
matter to the established crisis management structure without delay.

1. Purpose

(a) This guidance material should support aerodrome operators in the UAE in establishing,
implementing, and maintaining disruption management arrangements as a supplement to
existing crisis management frameworks. It should assist aerodrome operators in managing
abnormal, escalating, or prolonged operational conditions that may not immediately
constitute a crisis but may affect safety, security, capacity, passenger welfare, business
continuity, emergency preparedness, or stakeholder coordination.
(b) Disruption management should be positioned as the operational coordination layer between
routine airport operations and formal crisis management. It should provide a structured
mechanism for early identification, assessment, coordination, escalation, stabilisation, and
recovery during periods of operational variability or instability.

(c) This guidance material should be read in conjunction with applicable UAE civil aviation
regulations and aerodrome operator obligations, including the GCAA aerodrome regulatory
framework, aerodrome crisis management requirements, safety management system
requirements, business continuity arrangements, and airport emergency planning provisions.

2. Applicability

(a) This guidance material should apply to certificated aerodrome operators in the UAE and,
where appropriate, to aerodromes, heliports, helidecks, vertiports, and other aviation
infrastructure operators that may be required to maintain coordinated operational resilience
arrangements.
(b) Aerodrome operators should adapt the guidance proportionately, taking into account:
(1) the size, complexity, and operational category of the aerodrome;
(2) the volume and nature of aircraft operations;
(3) passenger and cargo throughput;
(4) critical infrastructure dependencies;
(5) diversion aerodrome roles;
(6) emergency response capability;
(7) stakeholder and contractor complexity; and
(8) national crisis and emergency management interfaces.

3. Definition

(a) For the purpose of this guidance material:
(1) Disruption management means the structured and coordinated capability through
which an aerodrome operator should anticipate, assess, manage, stabilise, escalate, and
recover from abnormal operational conditions that may affect safety, security, capacity,
passenger flow, business continuity, emergency readiness, stakeholder coordination, or
the continuity of critical aerodrome services.
(b) Disruption management should not replace crisis management, business continuity
management, emergency planning, safety management, security management, or airport
facilitation. It should function as an integrating operational mechanism that connects these
disciplines during abnormal operations.

4. Regulatory Intent

(a) The regulatory intent of disruption management should be to ensure that aerodrome
operators can maintain operational control during abnormal or escalating conditions before
such conditions develop into a crisis or cause unacceptable degradation of aerodrome safety,
security, continuity, or passenger welfare.
(b) Disruption management should enable aerodrome operators to:
(1) identify early indicators of operational instability;
(2) maintain a common operating picture;
(3) coordinate internal departments and external stakeholders;
(4) manage airport capacity and operational demand;
(5) protect passengers, staff, contractors, and visitors;
(6) maintain emergency response readiness;
(7) preserve critical aerodrome functions;
(8) support strategic escalation to crisis management where required; and
(9) coordinate recovery and return to normal operations.

5. Relationship with Crisis Management

(a) Disruption management should be established as a supplement to crisis management. It
should support the crisis management framework by providing the operational coordination,
information management, and escalation arrangements required before, during, and after
formal crisis activation.
(b) Crisis management should remain focused on strategic leadership, executive decision-making,
national-level coordination, regulatory liaison, public communication, reputational
management, and strategic recovery. Disruption management should remain focused on
operational stabilisation, stakeholder coordination, passenger and aircraft flow, resource
prioritisation, situation reporting, and escalation control.
(c) Aerodrome operators should ensure that disruption management arrangements include clear
thresholds for escalation to the crisis management team. These thresholds should include, as
a minimum:
(1) actual or potential impact on safety or security;
(2) degradation of critical aerodrome services;

(3) prolonged or escalating passenger congestion;
(4) significant airspace, runway, apron, terminal, or landside disruption;
(5) activation or potential activation of national crisis coordination arrangements;
(6) major reputational or media sensitivity;
(7) large-scale diversion, cancellation, or delay impacts;
(8) requirement for executive-level prioritisation; and
(9) loss of local operational control.

6. Relationship with Business Continuity Management

(a) Disruption management should be linked to the aerodrome operator’s business continuity
management arrangements. Business continuity should identify critical functions, minimum
operating capability, dependencies, alternative arrangements, recovery priorities, and
restoration strategies. Disruption management should apply these arrangements in real time
when abnormal conditions affect airport operations.
(b) Aerodrome operators should ensure that disruption management procedures support
continuity of:
(1) aerodrome operations control;
(2) airside operations;
(3) runway, taxiway, and apron availability;
(4) terminal processing;
(5) passenger information and welfare services;
(6) security screening and access control;
(7) baggage handling;
(8) ground handling coordination;
(9) emergency response services;
(10) communications and information systems;
(11) utilities and critical infrastructure; and
(12) command, coordination, and decision-making facilities.

7. Relationship with Emergency Planning

(a) Disruption management should be coordinated with the Airport Emergency Plan. Emergency
planning should remain focused on preparedness for events requiring emergency response,

life safety measures, incident command, evacuation, shelter-in-place, emergency medical
response, fire and emergency services deployment, and multi-agency incident management.
(b) Disruption management should support emergency planning by ensuring that abnormal
operational conditions do not compromise emergency response readiness. Aerodrome
operators should assess whether disruption conditions may affect:
(1) access and egress routes for emergency vehicles;
(2) emergency operations centre readiness;
(3) mobile command post deployment;
(4) fire and emergency services staffing and response capability;
(5) emergency communications;
(6) mass casualty management arrangements;
(7) shelter-in-place arrangements;
(8) evacuation routes and assembly areas;
(9) mutual-aid access and staging; and
(10) family assistance or humanitarian response requirements.
(c) Aerodrome operators should ensure that disruption management arrangements support
immediate transition to emergency response where a disruption escalates into an emergency
event.

8. Governance Requirements

(a) Aerodrome operators should establish a disruption management governance structure that
defines accountability, authority, coordination, escalation, and review arrangements.
(b) The governance structure should include:
(1) an accountable executive or senior responsible person;
(2) a designated disruption management lead or operational resilience lead;
(3) defined roles for the airport operations control centre;
(4) departmental responsibilities for airside, terminal, landside, security, safety, emergency
planning, facilities, communications, facilitation, and passenger services;
(5) coordination arrangements with airlines, ground handlers, ANSPs, government
agencies, contractors, and emergency services;
(6) defined escalation thresholds;
(7) decision-making authority limits;
(8) situation reporting requirements;

(9) documentation and record-keeping arrangements; and
(10) post-disruption review and improvement processes.
(c) Disruption management should be referenced in the Aerodrome Manual, Airport Emergency
Plan, Business Continuity Plan, Crisis Management Plan, relevant operational contingency
procedures, and stakeholder coordination protocols.

9. Disruption Management Framework

(a) Aerodrome operators should establish a disruption management framework based on the
following core elements:
9.1 Anticipation
(a) Aerodrome operators should identify reasonably foreseeable disruption scenarios that may
affect aerodrome operations. These should include, where applicable:
(1) regional conflict or airspace instability;
(2) mass diversions;
(3) prolonged flight delays or cancellations;
(4) runway, taxiway, or apron restrictions;
(5) terminal congestion;
(6) baggage system disruption;
(7) security-related disruption;
(8) cyber or IT system degradation;
(9) loss of utilities or critical infrastructure;
(10) severe weather;
(11) public health events;
(12) staff shortages or contractor failure;
(13) fuel supply disruption;
(14) emergency response resource degradation; and
(15) major passenger welfare impacts.
9.2 Monitoring
(a) Aerodrome operators should establish arrangements for continuous monitoring of
operational conditions. Monitoring should include indicators related to:
(1) aircraft movement rates;
(2) airspace restrictions;

(3) runway and taxiway availability;
(4) apron and stand capacity;
(5) passenger density;
(6) queue times;
(7) immigration, customs, and security processing capacity;
(8) baggage delivery performance;
(9) ground handler availability;
(10) emergency services readiness;
(11) infrastructure and system availability;
(12) staff fatigue and welfare; and
(13) stakeholder reports.
9.3 Assessment
(a) Aerodrome operators should assess disruption impacts using a structured risk-based method.
The assessment should consider safety, security, operational continuity, passenger welfare,
infrastructure, staffing, regulatory compliance, public communication, and recovery
implications.
9.4 Coordination
(a) Aerodrome operators should activate appropriate coordination mechanisms when disruption
thresholds are reached. These mechanisms should include operational briefings, stakeholder
coordination calls, situation reports, resource allocation meetings, and escalation to senior
leadership where required.
9.5 Response
(a) Aerodrome operators should implement proportionate response measures to stabilise
operations. Measures may include revised aircraft parking plans, terminal flow control,
dynamic staffing, passenger welfare support, contractor mobilisation, prioritisation of critical
flights, activation of overflow areas, revised ground transport arrangements, and enhanced
communication.
9.6 Recovery
(a) Aerodrome operators should define recovery objectives and return-to-normal criteria.
Recovery should include restoration of critical services, backlog management, passenger
reconciliation, staff relief, infrastructure inspection, stakeholder debriefing, and post-event
review.

10. Common Operating Picture

(a) Aerodrome operators should establish and maintain a common operating picture during
disruption. The common operating picture should provide a verified, shared, and current
understanding of the situation across airport functions and external stakeholders.
(b) The common operating picture should include:
(1) current operational status;
(2) safety and security considerations;
(3) airspace or traffic flow restrictions;
(4) runway, taxiway, apron, and stand availability;
(5) terminal processing status;
(6) passenger congestion areas;
(7) critical infrastructure status;
(8) emergency response readiness;
(9) stakeholder actions;
(10) current decisions and outstanding actions;
(11) escalation status; and
(12) recovery priorities.
(c) Aerodrome operators should ensure that the common operating picture is supported by
accurate reporting, clear ownership, agreed terminology, and regular update cycles.

11. Airport Operations Control Centre

(a) The Airport Operations Control Centre, or equivalent operational coordination facility, should
serve as the operational anchor for disruption management. It should coordinate operational
information, maintain the common operating picture, facilitate stakeholder coordination,
support escalation, and record operational decisions.
(b) The AOCC should be capable of:
(1) receiving and validating operational information;
(2) coordinating airport-wide operational responses;
(3) supporting real-time decision-making;
(4) communicating with airlines, ground handlers, ANSPs, government agencies,
emergency services, and contractors;
(5) monitoring capacity and demand;

(6) escalating to crisis management;
(7) coordinating passenger flow and welfare measures; and
(8) supporting recovery planning.

12. Passenger Welfare and Airport Facilitation

(a) Aerodrome operators should integrate passenger welfare and airport facilitation into
disruption management arrangements. Passenger welfare should not be treated only as an
airline responsibility where disruption creates airport-wide safety, congestion, welfare,
accessibility, or public order implications.
(b) Aerodrome operators should coordinate with airlines, ground handlers, immigration, security
agencies, commercial partners, and public transport providers to manage:
(1) passenger information;
(2) welfare support;
(3) vulnerable passenger assistance;
(4) accessible services;
(5) food, water, medical, and comfort provisions where appropriate;
(6) transfer passenger management;
(7) queue control;
(8) overflow areas;
(9) family and humanitarian assistance interfaces; and
(10) landside transport and kerbside congestion.
(c) Airport facilitation arrangements should support controlled passenger flow, efficient
processing, accessibility, way-finding, and communication during disruption.

13. Capacity and Demand Management

(a) Aerodrome operators should maintain procedures for assessing airport capacity against
operational demand during disruption. The procedures should consider airside, terminal,
landside, staffing, emergency response, infrastructure, and stakeholder capacity.
(b) Aerodrome operators should establish triggers for capacity management actions, including:
(1) aircraft stand saturation;
(2) runway or taxiway capacity reduction;
(3) terminal congestion;

(4) excessive queue times;
(5) baggage system backlog;
(6) ground handling constraints;
(7) reduced emergency response capability;
(8) landside access congestion; and
(9) prolonged passenger dwell times.
(c) Where demand exceeds available capacity, the aerodrome operator should coordinate
mitigating measures with airlines, ANSPs, ground handlers, government agencies, and other
relevant stakeholders.

14. Stakeholder and Contractor Coordination

(a) Aerodrome operators should ensure that stakeholders and contractors are integrated into
disruption management arrangements. Coordination should include airlines, ground handlers,
ANSPs, security agencies, immigration, customs, police, civil defence, ambulance services,
facilities management providers, fuel providers, transport operators, commercial tenants, and
other critical service providers.
(b) Aerodrome operators should maintain current contact lists, escalation pathways,
communication protocols, briefing arrangements, and operational expectations for each
relevant stakeholder group.
(c) Contractors providing safety-critical, security-critical, passenger-facing, infrastructure, or
continuity-related services should be included in disruption exercises and readiness reviews.

15. Mutual-Aid Emergency Services Interoperability

(a) Aerodrome operators should ensure that mutual-aid emergency services arrangements are
interoperable, documented, tested, and operationally practical.
(b) Mutual-aid interoperability should include:
(1) agreed command and coordination structures;
(2) common terminology;
(3) compatible or interoperable communications;
(4) access and escort arrangements;
(5) staging and rendezvous points;
(6) resource accountability;

(7) joint briefing arrangements;
(8) emergency response route protection;
(9) familiarisation with aerodrome layout and hazards; and
(10) post-incident demobilisation and recovery arrangements.
(c) Interoperability should be validated through exercises, briefings, and periodic readiness
checks.

16. Human Factors and Performance

(a) Aerodrome operators should consider human performance, staff safety and well-being as part
of disruption management. Prolonged disruption may increase fatigue, stress, workload,
communication burden, decision pressure, and the risk of reduced situational awareness.
(b) Aerodrome operators should establish arrangements for:
(1) workload monitoring;
(2) staff rotation and relief;
(3) fatigue management;
(4) welfare support;
(5) psychological safety;
(6) clear briefing and debriefing;
(7) supervision of safety-critical tasks;
(8) support for frontline passenger-facing personnel; and
(9) leadership visibility.
(c) Human factors considerations should be included in disruption exercises and post-event
reviews.

17. Information Management and Communication

(a) Aerodrome operators should establish communication arrangements for internal teams,
external stakeholders, passengers, media interfaces, and government coordination.
(b) Communication should be:
(1) timely;
(2) accurate;
(3) verified;
(4) consistent;

(5) accessible;
(6) coordinated; and
(7) proportionate to the disruption.
(c) Aerodrome operators should ensure that passenger-facing communications are aligned with
airline and government agency messaging, particularly during delays, cancellations, diversions,
security-related disruption, shelter-in-place activation, or major congestion.

