(a) Aerodrome Operator s are encouraged to provide the plans and diagrams in a format that will
permit the production of charts in accordance with the specifications of ICAO Annex 4.  The use of
graphical tools and geographically aligned and georeferenced to WGS84 is preferable.
(b) The size of the plans should be commensurate with the size and complexity of the aerodrome,
however ideally an A3 or A4 size drawing should be included in the Aerodrome Manual.  The scale
of the plans should be sufficiently large to show clearly all the elements listed from (d) to (i) below.

(c) Where, due to the size and complexity of the aerodrome, it is not practicable to include the plans
in the aerodrome manual, the aerodrome manual should contain a reference to the plan and its
location.
(d) Location Plan:
 Plan of the aerodrome location showing the aerodrome as expressly set aside for aerodrome
purposes, including any aerodrome facilities and equipment outside the boundaries of the
aerodrome.
(e) Boundary Plan:
 A plan showing the boundaries of the aerodrome and permanent survey points.  This would
normally be shown on a title deed.  If the boundaries of the aerodrome are not defined in the
documents of the title - provide details of claim to land or interest in,  the property on which the
aerodrome is located and a plan showing the boundaries and position of the aerodrome.
(f) Aerodrome Plan:
 Plan of the aerodrome showing the aerodrome facilities for the operation of the aerodrome
including (where applicable):
(1) Aerodrome Reference Point with elevation and geographical coordinate (WGS84) labelled;
(2) runways with dimensions labelled;
(3) runway surface types (concrete, asphalt, gravel, etc.) labelled;
 Note.- Bearing strengths or aircraft type restrictions may be shown in tabular form.
(4) runway elevations;
(5) runway strip with dimensions labelled;
(6) stopway with stopway end elevation;
(7) clearway with dimensions labelled, and clearway end elevations;
(8) Runway End Safety Area;
(9) approach lighting;

(10) taxiways with names;
(11) taxiway surface types;
 Note.-  Bearing strengths or aircraft type restrictions may be shown in tabular form.
(12) apron with names;
(13) navigational aids labelled with type (VOR, DME, etc.) showing critical and sensitive areas
identified where possible;
(14) localiser array aerials (with critical and sensitive areas shown);
(15) glide path aerials (with critical and sensitive areas shown);
(16) airside roads;
(17) terminal buildings;
(18) airport fire station(s);
(19) aerodrome control tower;
(20) power supply buildings;
(21) other main buildings relevant to the operation of the aerodrome;
(22) airside/landside perimeter fence;
(23) airside/landside perimeter gates (with gate numbers labelled);
(24) meteorological facilities including wind indicators;
(25) boundary of the air traffic control service; and
(26) any part of the movement area permanently unsuitable for aircraft and clearly marked as
such.
(g) Apron Plan:
plan of the apron areas including (where applicable):
(1) apron with identifying names;

(2) bearing strengths or aircraft type restrictions;
Note.-  Bearing strengths or aircraft type restrictions may be shown in tabular form.
(3) apron markings associated with the movement and parking of aircraft;
(4) aircraft parking bay designations clearly labelled;
(5) apron markings associated with the parking of vehicles and equipment;
(6) apron markings associated with the operation of vehicles;
(7) location of any nose in guidance system;
(8) any run–up bays or engine start points;
(9) the limits of the apron area;
(10) the boundary of the air traffic control service;
(11) any buildings that front onto the apron;
(12) access gates to the airside area;
(13) a table showing the maximum aircraft code, type, or size permitted to park on each aircraft
stand;
(14) Vertical Take-off and Landing Capable Aircraft  operating sites; and
(15) any part of the apron area permanently unsuitable for aircraft and clearly marked as such.
(h) Ground Movement Plan:
Plan of ground markings used for aircraft guidance showing:
(1) all runway markings;
(2) markings in pre-threshold areas; and
(3) taxiway and taxilane markings.
(i) Lighting Plan:
Plan of the airfield lighting showing, where applicable:

(1) approach lights;
(2) runway threshold lights;
(3) runway threshold identification lights;
(4) runway edge lighting;
(5) runway end lighting;
(6) stopway lights;
(7) visual landing aids (e.g. PAPI);
(8) runway guard lights;
(9) stop bar and intermediate taxiway holding position lighting;
(10) taxiway lighting;
(11) apron flood lighting;
(12) obstacle lights on the aerodrome; and
(13) illuminated windsocks;
(14) any other aeronautical ground lighting.

(a) An Aerodrome Certificate may be amended by the GCAA:
(1)  in response to a request by the Aerodrome Operator;
(2) as a consequence of enforcement action by the GCAA;
(3) if there is a change in the ownership or management of the aerodrome;
(4) if there is a change in the use or operation of the aerodrome;
(5) if there is a change in the boundaries of the aerodrome; and/or
(6) for administrative purposes to reflect the status of associated approvals or acceptance.
(b) The GCAA may amend an Aerodrome Certificate so as to restrict or prohibit specific operations if
the operator breaches the scope and conditions of the Aerodrome Certificate . The GCAA will
provide notice of intention to amend an Aerodrome Certificate stating the reasons for the proposed
