An operator intending to terminate the operation of an aerodrome, shall:
(a) notify the GCAA;
(b) provide such information to the appropriate Aeronautical Information Service provider;
(c) surrender the Aerodrome Certificate to the GCAA upon the date of termination of operation; and
(d) ensure that appropriate measures have been taken to avoid the unintended use of the aerodrome
by aircraft, unless the GCAA has approved the use of the aerodrome for other purposes.

(a) A Landing Area Acceptance may be amended by the GCAA:
(1) in response to a formal request by the Aerodrome Operator;
(2) as a consequence of enforcement action by the GCAA;
(3) If there is a change in the ownership or management of the aerodrome;
(4) If there is a change in the use or operation of the aerodrome;
(5) If there is a change in the boundaries of the aerodrome.
(b) The GCAA may amend a Landing Area Acceptance so as to restrict or prohibit specific operations if
the operator breaches the scope and conditions of the Landing Area Acceptance. The GCAA will
provide notice of intention to amend a Landing Area Acceptance st ating the reasons for the
proposed amendment.

An operator intending to terminate the operation of an aerodrome, shall:
(a) notify the GCAA;
(b) provide such information to the appropriate Aeronautical Information Service provider;

(c) surrender the Landing Area Acceptance to the GCAA upon the date of termination of operation;
and
(d) ensure that appropriate measures have been taken to avoid the unintended use of the aerodrome
by aircraft, unless the GCAA has approved the use of the aerodrome for other purposes.

(a) An activity or service is considered partially contracted when only specific tasks or components of a
broader aerodrome operational function are outsourced to another organisation.
Examples of partially contracted activities include, but are not limited to:
• pavement maintenance within the airfield maintenance programme;
• vegetation control;
• airfield ground lighting maintenance;
• rubber removal or surface friction treatment;
• wildlife control support services.
(b) An activity or service is considered fully contracted when the entire operational function under the
aerodrome operator’s scope is outsourced to a contracted organisation, including the responsibility for:
• operational management of the function;
• planning and execution of all related tasks;
• personnel and equipment;

• compliance with applicable regulatory requirements.
Examples of fully contracted activities may include:
• rescue and firefighting services;
• airside operations;
• aerodrome maintenance services (including pavement, lighting, markings, inspections, and related
infrastructure).
(c) Contracting a single technical task within a broader aerodrome function shall not be considered a fully
contracted activity, even if the contracted organisation performs that specific task independently.

(a) The Aerodrome Operator  shall establish procedures to prohibit:
(1) smoking within the movement area, other operational areas of the aerodrome, or areas of
the aerodrome where fuel or other flammable material is stored;
(2) display of an open flame or undertaking of an activity that would create a fire hazard within:
(i) areas of the aerodrome where fuel or other flammable material is stored;
(ii) the movement area or other operational areas of the aerodrome, unless authorised
by the Aerodrome Operator .
(b) The Aerodrome Operator  shall establish a fire prevention plan that:
(1) commensurate with the types of aircraft operations and other activities at the aerodrome;
(2) include sufficient trained and competent personnel, equipment and facilities to implement
the plan;
(3) contain sufficient procedures for the identification, control and prevention of fire hazards at
the aerodrome;
(4) contain sufficient procedures for periodic testing of the adequacy of the plan and for
reviewing the results in order to improve its effectiveness; and
(5) comply with all relevant requirements of CAR AGA PART ACM.

(a) Aerodrome operator should identify a person or a group of people, within the organisation, with
the responsibility and authority to:

(1) develop, implement and maintain the Quality Assurance System (QAS);
(2) manage the organisation’s internal audit programme;
(3) identify and record any findings or concerns, and the evidence necessary to confirm findings
or concerns;
(4) initiate, recommend, or provide solutions to findings or concerns through consultation with
the management owning the non-conforming process or activity;
(5) communicate and co-ordinate activities with external auditors;
(6) analyse the root causes of concerns and findings for presentation to management for a
review of trends and potential areas of concern, and
(7) conduct and record regular management reviews to ensure corrective and preventive actions
are addressed and closed out within a specific time.
(b) Aerodrome operator should develop, measure and monitor their own quality indicators.
 Some examples of typical quality indicators are:
(1) reports derived from the analysis and investigation of operational logs and records kept of
incidents, occurrences, accidents, and other safety indicators;
(2) root cause analysis from corrective and preventive action records;
(3) performance measurements of both the QMS and the organisation’s operation;
(c) Quality assurance procedures should:
(1) be concise and complete enough to be a useful guide for a user with the appropriate skills to
perform the task(s) within the procedure;
(2) state specifically how the organisation will address and meet the requirements of rules,
relevant legislation and regulations, or other regulatory requirements;
(3) be current and meet the requirements of referenced document(s);
(4) be accessible to all users of the process.

SAFETY PERFORMANCE MONITORING AND MEASUREMENT
An Aerodrome Operator  should establish defined levels of safety for the following safety events:
Accidents and incidents involving aircraft on the ground including, but not limited to the following:
(a) Runway Incursion - CAT A/CAT B;
(b) Runway Incursion - CAT C/CAT D;
(c) Manoeuvring Area Excursion - CAT A/CAT C (related to Runway);
(d) FOD incident resulting in aircraft damage (any category);
(e) Aircraft Damage - CAT A/CAT B;
(f) Accidents and incidents involving airside ground vehicles;
(g) Runway friction measurements;
(h) AGL reliability;
(i) FOD found on airport pavements and runway/taxiway strip areas;
(j) Bird and wildlife strike with aircraft (Bird & Wildlife Hazard - CAT A/CAT B); and
(k) Fuel spills and fuel handling accidents and incidents.

MONTHLY SAFETY PERFORMANCE ASSESSMENT
Aerodrome operators should conduct a monthly safety performance assessment and forward to the GCAA
by the last day of the month. The assessment should contain as a minimum:
(a) monthly traffic movement;
(b) the status of the current safety performance and alert levels;
(c) identification of risk elements within those incidents related to safety performance indicators and
targets;
(d) Aerodrome Operator’s response to any breached alert level, and
(e) a summary of actions taken to maintain or improve safety performance and a review of the
effectiveness of any actions in progress relating to the measured risks.

ANNUAL SAFETY PERFORMANCE ASSESSMENT
An annual assessment should be conducted that contains as a minimum:
(a) a summary of the monthly elements required;
(b) an evaluation of the controls implemented to manage safety performance and identified risks;
(c) a summary of actions taken to maintain or improve safety levels and a timeline for implementation
of identified improvements.

The Aerodrome Operator  holding a Landing Area Acceptance:
(a) may replace the safety review board with any other appropriate forums;
(b) may include SMS as a component of the aerodrome manual.

COORDINATION OF SAFETY PROCEDURES
Coordination and interface with the safety procedures of other relevant organisations that are active at
the aerodrome include, but is not limited to the following:
(a) aircraft operators;
(b) air navigation service providers;
(c) providers of apron management services;
(d) ground handling service providers;
(e) providers of services to persons with reduced mobility;
(f) aircraft maintenance organisations;
(g) flying training organisations;
(h) government authorities that operate on the movement area;
(i) as well as other organisations that perform activities independently at the aerodrome.

SAFETY PROGRAMMES —AERODROME SAFETY COMMITTEES
(a) Establishment of safety committees:
 Notwithstanding the establishment of a Local Runway Safety Team , The    aerodrome   operator
should also   establish   safety committees that takes into considering , Runway , taxiway, Apron
safety.
(b) Management of safety committees:
 The Safety Committees should be chaired by an Aerodrome Operator’s official.
(c) Composition of Safety Committees should include, but not limited to representatives of:
(1) airlines;
(2) aircraft ground handling services providers;

(3) Aerodrome Operator ;
(4) air navigation service provider(s).
(d) Tasks:
 The tasks of the Safety Committees should be:
(1) to receive and evaluate reports on operational safety issues;
(2) to receive reports and statistical information on accidents and incidents, and propose
solutions;
(3) to advise on manoeuvring area/apron safety issues such as:
(i) promotion of apron safety discipline;
(ii) FOD prevention;
(iii) developing measures for safe operations;
(iv) considering actions to resolve manoeuvring area/apron safety problems;
(v) apron equipment issues;
(vi) adherence to vehicle traffic issues;
(vii) new and/or updated safety instructions;
(viii) personal protective clothing/equipment issues;
(ix) methods to develop and promote apron safety awareness initiatives;
(x) proposed aerodrome works;
(xi) proposed changes/developments to the movement area;
(xii) standard operating procedures, etc.

LOCAL RUNWAY SAFETY TEAM
(a) Context:

 As part of its runway safety programme, the Aerodrome Operator  should establish and lead a Local
Runway Safety Team and act on local runway safety issues, including runway incursion and
excursion prevention.
(b) Local Runway Safety Team composition:
  Participation should include representatives from all interested parties with direct involvement in
runway operations at the aerodrome, including, but is not limited, to:
(1) aerodrome operations;
(2) aerodrome engineering and maintenance;
(3) air navigation service providers;
(4) aircraft operators that operate at the aerodrome;
(5) aerodrome rescue and firefighting services;
(6) pilot representation;
(c) Role:
 The role of the Local Runway Safety Team should be to advise the appropriate management on
potential runway safety issues, and to recommend mitigating measures.
(d) Tasks:
  The Local Runway Safety Team may have the following tasks:
(1) identification of potential runway safety issues, including the need for establishment of hot
spots or other problem areas at the aerodrome and the review of the relevant entries of the
AIP for accuracy;
(2) developing and running local awareness campaigns, at suitable periods, including at the
start of a busy season or before an unusual event, that focus on local issues, for example,
producing and distributing local hot spot maps, or other guidance material considered as
necessary; local awareness campaigns should be periodically refreshed to maintain
interest and operational awareness of the relevant personnel;

(3) monitoring the number, type and, the severity of runway incursions; disseminating safety
recommendations delivered from accident and incident investigation findings as well as
other relevant lessons learned e.g. from operational experience and best risk mitigation
practices; sharing good practices to prevent runway incursions or excursions;
(4) assisting in verifying that communications between air traffic controllers, or other Air
Traffic Services personnel, pilots, and vehicle drivers are satisfactory, or if any
improvements could be suggested;
(5) making observations on a regular basis in different weather and visibility conditions to assess
if all runway entrances and visual aids are adequate, correctly located and understandable
by all parties concerned, with no possible ambiguity of their meaning, or identify potential
aerodrome design issues;
(6) understanding the operating difficulties of personnel working in other areas, and
recommending areas for improvement; when reviewing operating procedures, it is
necessary to ensure that the procedures employed by different companies at the
aerodrome are integrated and effective, so as to minimise the risk of runway incursions.
Care should be taken when examining existing or proposed runway capacity enhancing
procedures or noise abatement schemes involving runway preferential systems;
(7) provide inputs for the development of joint, initial and recurrent, training programmes and
familiarisation on runway incursion and excursion prevention, for all relevant personnel
(vehicle drivers and other personnel operating on the manoeuvring area, pilots, Air Traffic
Services personnel); this may include visits to the manoeuvring area to increase awareness of
the aerodrome layout, markings, signs, position of anemometers etc., where this is
considered necessary;
(8) providing advice prior to the implementation of changes to the aerodrome, practices and
procedures to identify potential for runway incursion or excursion;
(9) assessing the effectiveness of implemented operational solutions periodically;
(10) development of joint training programmes on runway incursion prevention;
(11) monitor the number, type and severity of runway safety events including incursions and
excursions;

(12) periodically review airfield compliance issues, alternative means of compliance or deviations
related to the runway;
(13) review aerodrome charts showing hotspots for accuracy and revisions;
(14) develop strategies to manage and mitigate the risk from hot spots, depending on the case
and may include but not limiting to:
(i) additional visual aids, if necessary;
(ii) establishing alternate routes;
(iii) introducing changes to the design parts of the aerodromes; and
(iv) the mitigation of blind spots in the aerodrome control tower.

Further guidance materials on the on the composition and activities of an effective runway safety can be
found in
(a) ICAO Runway Safety Hand Book;
(b) European Action Plan for the Prevention of Runway Excursions (EAPPRE) - Appendix A Guidelines
for Local Runway Safety Teams;
(c) European Action Plan for the Prevention of Runway Incursions (EAPPRI).

HOT SPOTS
Strategies to manage and mitigate the risk from hot spots, depending on the case, may include, but are
not limited to:
(a) awareness campaigns;

(b) additional visual aids (signs, markings, and lighting);
(c) establishment of alternative routings;
(d) introducing changes to the design of parts of the aerodrome; and
(e) the mitigation of blind spots in the aerodrome control tower;
(f) training needs.
Aerodrome charts showing hot spots should be produced locally, checked regularly for accuracy, revised
as needed, distributed locally, and published in the AIP.

SAFETY REPORTING SYSTEM
(a) Safety reporting system — General
(1) An effective safety reporting system should include but  not limiting to , Aerodrome
Operator’s personnel, aircraft operators, ground handling service providers, air navigation
service providers, and any other organisation operating on the aerodrome, or providing
services at the aerodrome;
(2) The safety reporting system should include voluntary reporting possibilities intended for
safety hazards identified by the reporter, and that may have potential safety consequences;
(3) The Aerodrome Operator  should identify which events are mandatory to be reported;
(4) The Aerodrome Operator  should provide the means and the format for reporting which
should be such that meets the existing reporting requirements as established by the GCAA,
in terms of time, format, and required information to be reported;
(5) The safety reporting system should include , when requested by the reporter,  an
acknowledgement  for the submission of the report;
(6) The reporting process should be as simple as possible, and well documented, including details
as to what, how, where, whom, and when to report;
(7) Regardless of the source or method of submission, once the information is received, it should
be stored in a manner suitable for easy retrieval and analysis;
(8) Access to the submitted reports should be restricted to persons responsible for storing and
analysing them;
(9) The safety reporting system should include a feedback system to the reporting person, when
requested by the reporter , on the outcome of the occurrence analysis.
(b) Wildlife hazard reporting
(1) The Aerodrome Operator  should ensure that its safety reporting system specifically addresses
the requirement for all relevant third parties and all aerodrome personnel, to report to the
Aerodrome Operator  all wildlife strikes, and relevant identified hazards;

(2) The reporting of such third parties should be done irrespectively of any other requirements
according to which they have to report to the Aerodrome Operator .

(a) The Aerodrome Operator  shall establish an adequate system of record keeping, covering all its
activities undertaken.
(b) The format of the records shall be specified in the aerodrome manual.
(c) Records shall be stored in a manner that ensures protection from damage, alteration and theft.
(d) Records shall be kept for a minimum of five years, except that the below records shall be kept as
follows:
(1) the aerodrome compliance register , the alternative means of compliance in use for the
lifespan of the Aerodrome Certificate/ Landing Area Acceptance;
(2) arrangements with other organisations, atleast, for as long as such arrangements are in
effect;
(3) manuals of aerodrome equipment or systems employed at the aerodrome, for as long as they
are used at the aerodrome;
(4) safety assessment reports for the lifetime of the system/procedure/activity;

(5) personnel training, qualifications, and medical records as well as their proficiency checks, as
appropriate;
(6) previous versions of risk and hazard register for a duration of not less than five years from
the published date of the current version;
(7) driving authorisations and, where applicable, language proficiency certificates as
appropriate;
(8) vehicle authorisations and Aerodrome Operator ’s vehicle maintenance records as
appropriate.

CHANGES AFFECTING STAKEHOLDERS
Potential stakeholders may include the following:
(a) air navigation service providers;
(b) airport rescue and firefighting services;
(c) security;
(d) aircraft operators;
(e) ground handlers and other associated service providers
(f) maintenance organisations;
(g) regulatory bodies; and
(h) other utility service providers (eg: power suppliers).

INTRODUCTION OF NEW AIRCRAFT TYPES
Aerodrome operator should establish procedures to:

(a) assesses the aerodrome compatibility whenever introducing an aircraft type/model new to the
aerodrome irrespective of the size of the aircraft;
(b) assessment should include all elements as per AMC1 ADR.OPS.B.090(b);
(c) include all concerned stakeholders in conducting the assessment;
(d) in case of envisaged overload operations due to the new aircraft type, necessary pavement
assessment should be carried out as required in this CAR.

GENERAL - POST IMPLEMENTATION OF CHANGE
Post implantation review should include, but not limiting to, the following:
(a) effectiveness of the change management process;
(b) learnings from the process;
(c) effectiveness of the proposed risk controls;
(d) compliance to the regulatory requirements;
(e) any incidents during the process;
(f) any amendment to process or controls or new training needs.

INFORMATION AS A PART OF PROJECT NOTIFICATION
The Aerodrome operator should provide the following as a part of their project notification:

(a) Project coordinator:  details of the nominated Project Coordinator;
(b) Project brief:  based on the project scope including but not limited to the initial concept of
 operations, general compliance details, and details regarding management of works;
(c) Accountable Manager endorsement: evidence the project has been notified to and endorsed
 by the Accountable Manager;
(d) Location Map:  clearly indicating the location(s) of the proposed works, access routes and  staging
 areas;
(e) Indicative project plan and timelines:  available information and anticipated project timing;
(f) Impact assessment:  including but not limited to impacts to the obstacle limitation surface,
instrument flight procedures, navigational aids,  visual aids,  rescue firefighting services,
airside/landside  boundaries whether temporary or permanent; and
(g) Other information:  details as requested by the GCAA as part of the notification form or as directly
 requested from time to time.

INFORMATION TO BE PROVIDED AS A PART OF PROJECT NOTIFICATION
(a) The Aerodrome Operator’s process should ensure that all projects are properly planned, managed
and coordinated according to documented and controlled procedures which include:
(1) elements required to define the project scope including:
(i) concept of operations if applicable;
(ii) impact assessment  of the project on aerodrome operations and compliance to
standards either temporary during works or permanently as a result of the project;
(iii) scope of physical works and identification of relevant standards;
(iv) known location(s) including works, access routes and staging areas;
(v) relationship and alignment to the aerodrome masterplan; and
(vi) indicative project plan and timelines including required approvals and acceptances;

(2) notification of the project scope to and endorsement by the accountable manager  prior to
commencement of the project including budgeting or investment approvals;
(3) nomination of a project coordinator, who will act as the focal point for coordination with the
GCAA.

An Aerodrome Operator  shall apply and obtain through means established by the GCAA, a special
operational approval when the Aerodrome Operator  plans to introduce:
(a) one-time provision of service;
(b) operations trials and testing;
(c) emerging new technology.

(a) One-time provision of service can include the following:
(1) use of aerodrome by aircraft exceeding the certified design characteristics of the  aerodrome;
(2) aerodrome infrastructure that is completed , however awaiting a technical acceptance;
(3) aerodrome infrastructure that is completed, however awaiting operational approval;
(4) air shows including static display of aircraft;
(b) Emerging new technology which  include  EVTOLs operations, autonomous vehicles operations, etc.

After receipt of a notification of findings, the Aerodrome Operator  shall:
(a) ensure that all audit reports raised by the GCAA are signed by the Accountable Manager within the
stipulated time;
(b) identify the root cause of the findings;
(c) provide a corrective action plan that meets the acceptance of the GCAA;
(d) demonstrate the corrective action implementation to the satisfaction of the GCAA within the period
agreed.

ENFORCEMENT ACTION
Enforcement action may be taken in response to:
(a) a violation against federal laws;
(b) demonstration of gross negligence, incompetence, or evidence of wilful act, sabotage;
(c) failure to give the GCAA access to organisation’s facilities or records;
(d) falsification of documentary evidence;
(e) malpractice or fraudulent use of the organisation or personal certificate / approval; or
(f) the lack of an Accountable Manager in an organisation.

AERODROME DATA
(a) Data relevant to the aerodrome and available services should include, but may not be limited to
the  items in the following list:
(1) aerodrome reference point;
(2) aerodrome and runway elevations;
(3) aerodrome reference temperature;
(4) aerodrome dimensions and related information;
(5) the bearing strength of the pavements;
(6) pre-flight altimeter check location;
(7) declared distances;
(8) intersection departure declared distances;
(9) condition of the movement area and related facilities;
(10) disabled aircraft removal capabilities;
(11) rescue and firefighting; and
(12) visual approach slope indicator systems.
(b) The Aerodrome Operator  should measure and report to the aeronautical information services
obstacles and terrain data in Area 3, and in Area 2 (the part within the aerodrome boundary) in
degrees, minutes, seconds and tenths of seconds. In addition, the top elevation, type, marking and
lighting (if any) of obstacles should be reported to the aeronautical information services.

(c) Electronic obstacle data for all obstacles in Area 2 (the part within the aerodrome boundary) that
are assessed as being a hazard to air navigation should be provided.
(d) Electronic terrain and obstacle data should be provided for:
(1) Area 2a, for those that penetrate the relevant obstacle data collection surface;
(2) penetrations of the take-off flight path area obstacle identification surfaces; and
(3) penetrations of the aerodrome obstacle limitation surfaces.
(e) Electronic terrain and obstacle data should be provided for Area 4 for terrain and obstacles that
penetrate the relevant obstacle data collection surface, for all runways where precision approach
Category II or III operations have been established and wher e detailed terrain information is
required by operators to enable them to assess the effect of terrain on decision height
determination by use of radio altimeters.
(f) The Aerodrome Operator  should establish arrangements with the Appropriate Authority for the
provision of obstacles and terrain data outside of the aerodrome boundary.
(g) The geographical coordinates of obstacles in Area 2 (the part within the aerodrome boundary) and
in Area 3 should  be measured and reported to the aeronautical information services provider in
degrees, minutes, seconds and tenths of seconds. In addition, the top elevation, type, marking and
lighting (if any) of obstacles shall be reported to the aeronautical information services provider.

CONDITION OF THE MOVEMENT AREA AND RELATED FACILITIES
The condition of the movement area and the operational status of related facilities need to be monitored
and reported, on matters of operational significance affecting aircraft and aerodrome operations,
particularly in respect of the following:
(a) construction or maintenance work;
(b) rough or broken surfaces on a runway, taxiway or apron;
(c) other temporary hazards, including parked aircraft;
(d) failure or irregular operation of part or all the aerodrome visual aids; and
(e) failure of the normal or secondary power supply.

VISUAL APPROACH SLOPE INDICATOR SYSTEMS
The following information concerning a visual approach indicator system should be made available:
(a) associated runway designation number;
(b) type of system; for a PAPI or APAPI installation, the side of the runway on which the lights are
installed, i.e. left or right, is given;
(c) where the axis of the system is not parallel to the runway centre line, the angle of displacement
and the direction of displacement, i.e. left or right, is indicated;

(d) nominal approach slope angle(s) For a PAPI and an APAPI this shall be angle (B + C) ÷ 2 and (A + B)
÷ 2, respectively as in Figure CS-M- 8:Light beams and angle of elevation setting for PAPI and APAPI;
and
(e) minimum eye height(s) over the threshold of the on -slope signal(s). For a PAPI this shall be the
setting angle of the third unit from the runway minus 2’, i.e. angle B minus 2’, and for an APAPI this
shall be the setting angle of the unit farther from the runway minus 2’, i.e. angle A minus 2’.

The Aerodrome Operator  shall have formal arrangements with   the  organisations  with  which  it
exchanges aeronautical data or aeronautical information and shall ensure the following:

(a) all data relevant to the aerodrome and available services is provided with the required quality; data
quality requirements (DQRs) are complied with at data origination and maintained during data
transmission;
(b) the accuracy of aeronautical data is as specified in the aeronautical data catalogue;
 Note.- The Aeronautical Data Catalogue is a general description of the aeronautical Information
management (AIM) data scope and consolidates all data that can be collected and maintained by
the aeronautical information service (AIS). It provides a reference for aeronautical data origination
and publication requirements;
(c) the integrity of aeronautical data is maintained throughout the data process from survey/
origination to transmission, to next intended user, based on the integrity classification specified in
the aeronautical data catalogue. In addition, procedures shall be put in place so that:
(1) for routine data, corruption is avoided throughout the processing of the data;
(2) for essential data, corruption does not occur at any stage of the entire process and additional
processes are included, as needed, to address potential risks in the overall system
architecture to ensure data integrity at that level;
(3) for critical data, corruption does not occur at any stage of the entire process and additional
integrity assurance processes are included to fully mitigate the effects of faults identified by
thorough analysis of the overall system architecture as potential data integrity risks;
(d) the resolution of the aeronautical data is commensurate with the actual data accuracy;
(e) the traceability of the aeronautical data;
(f) the timeliness of the aeronautical data, including any limits on the effective period;
(g) the completeness of the aeronautical data;
(h) the format of the delivered data meets the specified requirements;
(i) ensure that data validation and verification techniques are employed to ensure that the
aeronautical data meets the associated data quality requirements;
(j) data integrity should be assured by employing cryptographic technologies.

 Note.- The means to implement systematic cycling codes include the use of hash functions and
cyclic redundancy check (CRC).

FORMAL ARRANGEMENTS
(a) Organisations concerned
 The Aerodrome Operator  should have formal arrangements with public or private entities
providing:
(1) air navigation services;
(2) services for the origination and provision of survey data;
(3) electronic terrain data; and
(4) electronic obstacle data.
 with which it exchanges aeronautical data and/or aeronautical information.
(b) Content of formal arrangements
Such formal arrangements should include the following minimum content:
(1) aeronautical data to be provided;
(2) the quality requirements for each data item supplied according to the aeronautical data
catalogue;

(3) the method(s) for demonstrating that the data provided conforms with the specified
requirements;
(4) the action to be taken in the event of discovery of a data error, or inconsistency in any data
provided;
(5) the following minimum criteria for notification of data changes:
(i) criteria for determining the timeliness of data provision based on the operational or
safety significance of the change;
(ii) any prior notice of expected changes; and
(6) the means to be adopted for notification;
(7) the party responsible for documenting data changes;
(8) data exchange details such as format or format change process;
(9) any limitations on the use of data;
(10) requirements for the production of data origination quality reports;
(11) metadata to be provided; and
(12) contingency requirements concerning the continuity of data provision.

