TRAINING FOR AIRSIDE DRIVING PERMIT TRAINING PROVIDERS
The following elements should be considered when developing training programs and knowledge
requirements for all categories of airside vehicle drivers:
(a) Airside Driving Permit (ADP)
(1) the issuing authority, the validity of the permit in terms of time, conditions of use, and
its transferability;
(2) ownership of the permit and control, and audit of permit issue;
(3) local enforcement, and driving offence procedures; and
(4) relationship to UAE driver licensing system.
(b) National legislation and regulation
(1) UAE regulations related to general vehicle driving licenses;
(2) Local transport authority requirements; and
(3) GCAA requirements/guidance for driving airside.
(c) Aerodrome regulations and requirements
(1) ATC procedures that are applicable for vehicles;
(2) the definition of movement areas, manoeuvring areas, aprons and stands;
(3) specific aerodrome regulations, requirements, and local instructions;
(4) local methods used to disseminate general information, and instructions to drivers; and
(5) local methods used to disseminate information regarding works in progress.
(d) Personal responsibilities
(1) agreed aerodrome requirements concerning fitness to drive (medical and health
standards);
(2) issue and use of personal protective equipment, such as high visibility clothing and
hearing protection;
(3) general driving standards;
(4) no-smoking/no-alcohol, use of psychoactive substances and medicines requirements
airside;
(5) responsibilities with respect to foreign object debris and fuel/oil spillage; and
(6) the responsibility to ensure that a vehicle is suitable for the task, and is used correctly;
(7) Implementation of ‘sterile-cab’ concept, avoiding disturbing and distracting activities
while driving;

(8) requirements concerning fitness to drive (medical standards);
(9) responsibilities with respect safety reporting; and
(10) responsibilities with respect to escorting other vehicles (applicable for manoeuvring
area.
(e) Vehicle standards
(1) condition and maintenance standards – fit for purpose and task;
(2) requirements for daily inspection and inspection prior to operating on the manoeuvring
area;
(3) the requirement to display obstacle lights and company insignia;
(4) the requirement for, and content of, daily vehicle inspections;
(5) agreed standards of aerodrome and company vehicle fault reporting and rectification;
and
(6) requirements for the issue and display of airside vehicle permits.
(7) serviceability of all essential communication systems with air traffic services and base
operations.
(f) General aerodrome layout
(1) the geography of the aerodrome;
(2) aviation terminology used such as runway, taxiway, apron, roads, crossings, runway-
holding points;
(3) all aerodrome signs, markings and lighting for vehicles and aircraft; particular emphasis
on signs, markings and lighting used on the manoeuvring area and used to protect the
runway;
(4) specific reference to signs, markings and lighting used to guard runways, and critical
areas;
(5) specific reference to any controlled/uncontrolled taxiway crossing procedures;
(g) Airside traffic rules
(1) general rules;
(2) local rules;
(3) rules for operating in low visibility;
(4) speed limits, prohibited areas and no parking regulations;
(5) right of way of aircraft, towed aircraft, and rescue and fire fighting vehicles in an
emergency;
(6) reversing procedure.

(h) Hazards of general airside driving
(1) the danger zones around aircraft;
(2) taxiway / taxi lane crossings;
(3) engine suction/ingestion and jet blast, propellers and helicopters;
(4) aircraft refuelling;
(5) foreign object debris and spillages;
(6) vehicle reversing;
(7) staff and passengers walking across aprons;
(8) air bridges and other services such as fixed electrical ground power;
(9) the general aircraft turnaround process;
(10) aircraft emergency stop and fuel cut-off procedures;
(11) hazardous cargo;
(12) local vehicle towing requirements;
(13) requirements for driving at night;
(14) requirements for driving in adverse weather conditions, particularly low visibility;
(15) procedures in the event of a vehicle or radio becoming unserviceable while on the
manoeuvring area.
(i) Security procedures
(1) personal requirements (identification cards) and exemptions where applicable;
(2) vehicle security permits;
(3) security restricted areas;
(4) security critical areas.
(j) Penalties for non-compliance
(1) Human performance, including:
(2) basic concepts of human factors;
(3) basic aviation psychology, including:
(i) attention and vigilance;
(ii) perception;
(iii) memory;
(iv) human error;
(v) decision-making;
(vi) avoiding and managing errors;
(vii) human behavior.

(k) Local organisations
(1) the role of the aerodrome operator in setting and maintaining standards;
(2) the GCAA and its responsibilities;
(3) the national and/or local police, and their involvement with airside driving;
(4) other enforcement authorities dealing with vehicles, driving, health, and safety.
(5) the role of the local ATS unit.
(l) Emergency procedures
(1) actions and responsibilities in a crisis situation (any accident or significant incident
occurring on the aerodrome);
(2) action in the event of a vehicle accident, in the event of a vehicle striking an aircraft,
FOD events;
(3) action in the event of fire;
(4) procedures to be used by vehicle drivers if lost or unsure of their position;
(5) action in the event of an aircraft accident/incident;
(6) action in the event of personal injury;
(7) emergency telephone numbers.
(m) Communications
(1) radio procedures and phraseologies to be used if applicable;
(2) light signals used by ATC;
(3) procedures to be used by vehicle drivers if lost or unsure of position;
(4) local emergency telephone numbers; and
(5) how to contact the local aerodrome safety unit.
(n) Practical training (visual familiarisation)
(1) airside service roads, taxiway crossings, and any restrictions during low visibility;
(2) aprons and stands- boundary of responsibility line , stand safety lines
(3) surface paint markings for vehicles and aircraft;
(4) surface paint markings that delineate the boundary between aprons and taxiways;
(5) parking areas and restrictions;
(6) pushback line , pushback limit lines
(7) vehicle limit lines;
(8) pedestrian crossing;
(9) equipment staging area;
(10) airbridge wheel positions;

(11) underground services, including fuel hydrant markings;
(12) FOD bins;
