CORE TRAINING AND PROFICIENCY CHECK AREAS
(a) For operations manager, training and proficiency checks programmes should include, as a
minimum, the following areas:
(1) roles and responsibilities as a VFI operations manager;
(2) GCAA regulations on vertical flight infrastructures (heliport, helideck or vertiport);
(3) safety management system including human factors;
(4) aerodrome safeguarding – obstacle limitations, airspace management, etc.
(5) emergency planning and rescue and firefighting requirements;
(6) quality assurance and compliance monitoring; and
(7) facility maintenance programme.

(b) For landing officer and assistant, training and proficiency checks programmes should include,
as a minimum, the following areas:
(1) roles and responsibilities as VFI landing officer and assistant;
(2) safe and efficient movement of aircraft during landing, takeoff, and ground operations;
(3) visual and electronic aids used for aircraft guidance and landing;
(4) radio communication with air traffic control (ATC), pilots, and ground crew;
(5) hazard and risk assessment, including FOD (foreign object debris) and equipment
malfunctions;
(6) weather systems, interpreting meteorological data, and communicating conditions to
pilots; and
(7) emergency response procedures including coordinating with emergency services and
operations teams during abnormal or emergency scenarios.
(c) For RFF team leader and member, training and proficiency checks programmes should
include, as a minimum, the following areas:
(1) roles and responsibilities as VFI RFF team leader and member;
(1) command, control and coordination with internal and external agencies/services during
emergencies;
(2) applications of principal and complementary extinguishing agents for different aircraft
hazards;
(3) use of rescue and firefighting vehicles and equipment provided at the facility;
(4) use of personal protective clothing and respiratory protective equipment for different
emergency scenarios;
(5) firefighting tactics and techniques in dealing with emergencies involving different types
of aircraft - both fuel-based and alternative-fuel types;
(6) search and rescue operations of casualties, including applying emergency first aid and
CPR; and
(7) handling hazardous materials, including aviation fuel and battery fire hazards.
(d) For radio operators, training and proficiency checks programmes should include, as a
minimum, the following areas:
(1) roles and responsibilities as VFI radio operators;
(2) GCAA regulations on radio communication protocols, including phraseology and timing;
(3) handling communications during emergencies, ensuring timely coordination between
aircraft, ground personnel and air traffic control;

(4) communication equipment testing and inspection to ensure reliability during
operations; and
(5) maintain situational awareness of operational status and conveying critical information
to relevant parties.
(e) For audit team leader and member, training and proficiency checks programmes should
include, as a minimum, the following areas:
(1) roles and responsibilities as VFI audit team leader and member;
(2) GCAA regulations on vertical flight infrastructures (heliport, helideck or vertiport);
(3) safety management system including quality assurance and compliance monitoring;
(4) safety audit management system; and
(5) safety investigation.

OVERSIGHT ACTIVITIES
(a) Oversight activities by the GCAA consist of the acceptance process of the training provider and
the continued surveillance of the training provider’s training delivery after acceptance. The
purpose of the surveillance activities is to ensure that the training provider is operating within
the terms of its acceptance and as described in the training and competency manual.
(b) The GCAA’s oversight activities include a review of the training provider’s quality assurance
(QA) system, its administrative, technical and training records and its operational activities.
Surveillance is an ongoing function that may also include consideration of records held by the
GCAA, for example, practical test and examination results, in addition to on-site inspections,
audits and other surveillance activities.
(c) The main elements of the training provider activities that are subject to the GCAA’s oversight
include, as applicable, the following:
(1) staff adequacy in terms of number and qualifications;
(2) validity of assessors’ and instructors’ certificates and authorizations;
(3) logbooks;
(4) appropriate and adequate facilities for the training and for the number of students;
(5) documentation process (e.g., the review and update of the training and competency
manual), with particular emphasis on course documentation, including records of
updates, training/operations manuals, etc.;
(6) training delivery in the classroom and in simulation devices and, if applicable,
instruction or on the-job training, including briefing and de-briefing;
(7) instructor and assessor training;
(8) QA practices;
(9) safety management functionality;
(10)  training, examination and assessment records;
(11)  evaluation and checking;
(12)  equipment serviceability;
(13)  maintenance records; and

(14)  training device qualification and acceptance.

